    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d778">

                <pb facs="0726" n="554" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp01v01/pg554"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/3567</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Morris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-03-21T18:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 21, 1917, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received March 22, 3 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>246. Referring to the Department’s 137<anchor n="1" xml:id="d778-f1"/> and
                    circular March 16, 2 p.m. Foreign Minister told me to-day he was authorized give
                        <pb facs="0727" n="555" xml:id="pg555"/> verbal Swedish neutrality rules.
                    Contained no mention of armed merchant vessels, and that for the present Swedish
                    Government was unable make any definite decision, but reserved the right to
                    treat each case separately later in conference. I drew from Foreign Minister the
                    statement that for the present armed American merchant vessels, whether armed by
                    the Government or by the owners, would be allowed freely to enter and depart
                    from Swedish ports as heretofore. In reply to my inquiry the Minister for
                    Foreign Affairs <hi rendition="#italic">confidentially</hi> stated that Sweden
                    did not care to set a precedent on this question at present, but preferred to
                    await developments, and that in not definitely committing themselves at present,
                    Sweden obviated what might lead to some embarrassment with neighboring
                    countries. I learned to-day from high Swedish official that Danish
                    representative will make a similar reply.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Morris</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
